.TH NESDIT 1 "April 2026" "nesdit 0.1.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nesdit \- edit structured config files with jq-style queries
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nesdit
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIFILE\fR...]
.PP
.B nesdit
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fB\-\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nesdit
reads a single JSON, YAML, or TOML document, applies a jq-style query,
and writes the result to stdout or back to the file.
.PP
Supported input formats are JSON, YAML, and TOML; the format is detected
from the file extension by default and may be overridden with
.BR \-\-format .
Output preserves key order for YAML and TOML documents.
.PP
Queries follow jq syntax. String variable bindings
.RB ( \-\-arg ,
.BR \-\-argjson )
and query-file loading
.RB ( \-\-from\-file )
are supported.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Run modes
.TP
.B \-\-edit
Open
.I $EDITOR
on a temporary copy of the file, then emit a suggested query derived from
the diff of the before and after states.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-in\-place
Edit the file(s) in-place using atomic temp-and-rename writes.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Emit a unified diff of the before and after states to stdout; do not write
any file.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Exit 2 if the query would change the input, exit 0 if the result is
identical, exit 1 on error.  Useful for CI drift detection.
.SS Output control
.TP
.BI \-\-output\-format " format"
Force the output format:
.IR json ,
.IR yaml ,
or
.IR toml .
Defaults to the same format as the input.
When the output format is
.B toml
and more than one document is written to the stream, documents are separated
by
.B +++
(a line containing only three plus signs).
This convention follows Hugo-style multi-document TOML and is not part of the
TOML specification.
.TP
.B \-\-pretty
Emit human-readable TOML output: arrays with more than one element are
expanded across multiple lines with 2-space indentation, inline tables
with more than one key are expanded to block form, and blank lines are
inserted between top-level entries.
Silently ignored for non-TOML output formats (JSON, YAML, JSONL).
.TP
.BI \-\-log\-format " mode"
Stderr rendering mode:
.B text
(default, one canonical line per event) or
.B json
(NDJSON, one JSON object per event).
.SS Query control
.TP
.BI \-\-query " query"
A jq-style query string.  Defaults to the identity filter
.RB ( . )
when neither
.B \-\-query
nor
.B \-f
is given.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-from\-file " \fIpath\fR"
Load the query from a file.  Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-query .
.TP
.BI \-\-arg " K=V"
Bind
.I $K
to the literal string
.I V
in the query.  Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-argjson " K=V"
Bind
.I $K
to the JSON-decoded value
.I V
in the query.  Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-where " predicate"
Filter documents by a jq predicate before applying the main query.  In
stream mode, non-matching documents pass through unchanged.  In file mode,
non-matching documents are skipped.
.TP
.B \-\-create\-missing
Allow queries to create keys or paths that do not exist in the input
document.  By default, absent paths are rejected.
.SS Format options
.TP
.BI \-\-format " format"
Force the input format:
.IR json ,
.IR jsonl ,
.IR yaml ,
or
.IR toml .
Default is extension-based detection.
.TP
.BI \-\-yaml\-version " version"
YAML boolean coercion dialect:
.B 1.1
treats
.IR yes / no / on / off
as booleans;
.B 1.2
(default) treats only
.IR true / false
as booleans.
.SS Resource caps
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-bytes " N"
Reject inputs larger than
.I N
bytes.  0 disables the cap.  Default: 10485760 (10 MiB).
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-depth " N"
Reject documents nested deeper than
.I N
levels.  0 disables the cap.  Default: 1000.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-yaml\-nodes " N"
YAML node-materialisation cap (billion-laughs mitigation).  0 disables
the cap.  Default: 100000.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-query\-bytes " N"
Reject query files loaded via
.B \-\-from\-file
larger than
.I N
bytes.  0 disables the cap.  Default: 1048576 (1 MiB).
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " duration"
Cancel the query after
.I duration
(e.g.\&
.IR 500ms ,
.IR 30s ).
0 disables the cap.
.SS Global flags
.TP
.B \-\-backup[=\fIsuffix\fR]
Write a sibling backup file before each in-place edit.  Default suffix is
.IR .bak ;
a custom suffix may be given as
.BR \-\-backup=.orig .
Requires
.BR \-i .
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-going
Continue processing after per-document errors; errored documents are
skipped.  Exits 1 at the end if any errors occurred.  Default behaviour
halts on the first error.
.TP
.B \-\-strict
Halt on the first document error.  This is the default behaviour; this
flag is an explicit alias provided for documentation purposes.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help and exit.
.SH UTF-8 INSPECTION
Whole-body text inspection is available in the current development source;
this documents source capability, not a new published release.
.TP
.B --input-file FILE
Read exactly one nonempty regular-file path. Commas are literal; repeated files,
- as a stdin alias, and positional inputs are rejected. Symlinks resolving to
regular files are allowed. File mode never reads stdin or writes the source.
.TP
.B --input-stdin
Read one whole body through EOF. Must be explicitly true and supplied once;
mutually exclusive with input-file. False and repeated selections fail.
.TP
.B --input-codec text
Required for local text inspection. No charset, extension or content guessing.
The complete body must be valid UTF-8; invalid bytes fail before filtering.
Empty input becomes an empty string. BOM, NUL, CR/LF and whitespace remain content.
There is no trimming, line framing, arbitrary-byte fallback or raw-text export.
.TP
.B --to json
Optional explicit JSON target for text and MessagePack inspection.
MessagePack also permits explicit --to msgpack; see conversion below.
.PP
Options and queries are validated before payload reads. Use --filter and --project
against the original string root. A filter returns exactly one boolean; false
succeeds with zero output and skips projection. Projection returns exactly one
JSON value. Without projection the string bypasses identity normalization.
Only source, codec, target, filter, project, log-format and normal help/version
are allowed. Orphaned flags, legacy query/edit/format/presentation options,
Protobuf/native settings, timers and resource overrides fail even at defaults.
.PP
Local output is one JSON value plus LF, no envelope or banner. Unix terminals
use two-space pretty layout; redirected output is compact/plain. NO_COLOR and
TERM=dumb disable color, not indentation. Payload controls are JSON-escaped.
Windows output remains plain/compact.
.PP
The inclusive raw cap is 10 MiB. At the cap EOF or a next byte is required;
byte cap+1 fails immediately. An unfinished producer may wait indefinitely:
no local overall timer is provided. One depth-zero string also fits decoded/view
limits of depth 100, 1000000 nodes and 16 MiB aggregate text. Queries retain
1 MiB inline and depth 1000/1000000 nodes/16 MiB result limits. Exact JSON-size
preflight counts escaping, punctuation, indentation and LF against 16 MiB before
encoding; a valid body may expand past it. Filters run before output preflight,
but cannot hide invalid input. These are not hard RSS/CPU bounds or a query sandbox.
.PP
Native --nats-codec text uses the same whole-string decoder. Queries prepare before
connection. Existing envelope, provenance, count-before-filter, duration, TLS/auth
and permissions stay unchanged. Full-envelope JSON preflight includes metadata
and layout. Ordinary empty Core data becomes an empty string; bodyless status
failures remain errors. History empty data becomes an empty string and stored
Status headers remain data. Descriptor options are forbidden with text. History
retains NATS 2.15.x/default-route/homogeneous-deployment and INFO/GET-only rules.
.PP
Staging completes before external writing: pre-write failure emits no value,
while write/short-write failure may leave partial output. Never redirect over
the input: the shell may truncate it before nesdit starts. On Linux/macOS one
interrupt requests stopping, with the existing one-second process fallback even
for blocked I/O/diagnostics and invalid options after a parsed source selector.
Forced exit can truncate output or omit cleanup. Embedded Execute remains
cooperative: no host exit, borrowed closes/deadline changes or abandoned blocked
worker. Callers must unblock I/O. No new Windows bounded-stop promise.
Success/false filter exits 0, failure 1, cancellation 130. Diagnostics identify
stages/categories without body, query or evaluator contents. See the text guide.
.SH MESSAGEPACK INSPECTION AND CONVERSION
Available in development source; no new published release is announced.
.B nesdit --input-file event.msgpack --input-codec msgpack
or pipe one body to
.B nesdit --input-stdin --input-codec msgpack
with optional --to json, or explicit --to msgpack for binary stdout.
Ordinary --input-codec json and rich --input-codec msgpack-json require explicit
--to msgpack. Text remains JSON-only. Source isolation, query and stop/ownership
behavior follow local text inspection above. No broker MessagePack, FlatBuffers,
JSONL, direct YAML/TOML conversion or automatic codec detection.
.PP
For example, read ordinary.json with --input-codec json --to msgpack and redirect
to a separate event.msgpack. Read event.view.json with --input-codec msgpack-json
--to msgpack to reconstruct its semantic value. Ordinary tag-looking objects
stay application maps; the explicit rich source interprets tags.
.PP
JSON import requires a single value followed only by JSON whitespace. Invalid
UTF-8, escapes, lone surrogates and duplicate decoded object keys fail. Rich tags
must have exactly one known key and exact shape/arity; bare numbers are permitted
only as extension IDs and nanoseconds. Full source admission precedes filtering.
.PP
Exactly one complete MessagePack value and EOF are required. Empty input,
reserved code 0xc1, truncation, a second value or any trailing byte (even LF)
fails before filtering. Empty encoded strings, arrays, maps and binary are valid.
.PP
MessagePack JSON v1 uses null, booleans, valid UTF-8 strings and arrays directly.
Every map becomes {"$map":[[key,value],...]}, retaining order, duplicates and
compound keys. A literal application map {"$int":"7"} therefore displays as
{"$map":[["$int","7"]]}, not as a typed integer.
Integers use {"$int":"decimal"} in -2^63 through 2^64-1, with no plus,
leading zeros, exponent or integer negative zero. Signed-positive opcodes and
fixints of equal value have equal views; opcode/width identity is not preserved.
.PP
Floats use {"$float":"binary64-roundtrip"}. Float32 widens exactly:
float32 near 0.1 displays 0.10000000149011612, versus float64's 0.1.
Special strings are -0, NaN, Infinity and -Infinity; positive zero is 0.
This identifies IEEE values, not exact rational decimals or NaN payload bits.
Binary uses {"$bin":"base64"}; invalid-UTF-8 str uses {"$str64":"base64"},
never replacement characters or bin coercion. Base64 is padded and canonical.
Opaque extensions use {"$ext":[signed-ID,"base64"]} for IDs -128 through 127
except -1, including reserved negatives. Timestamp -1 uses
{"$time":["seconds",nanoseconds]} with signed-int64 seconds and nanos 0-999999999.
Valid 4/8/12-byte payloads and equivalent headers are accepted. Invalid timestamp
length/nanos fails, never opaque fallback; no RFC3339 range normalization.
Encoding uses minimal integers and shortest 4/8/12-byte timestamps. All Floats
encode as float64 (0xcb), with canonical quiet NaN bits 0x7ff8000000000000.
Typed finite Float strings permit decimal/exponent syntax and round nearest-even;
hex, separators and whitespace fail. Base64 import rejects whitespace, missing
padding and bad pad bits; str64 accepts only invalid-UTF-8 bytes.
.PP
Ordinary integer lexemes remain exact within -2^63 through 2^64-1.
Fraction/exponent tokens select binary64 rounding (0.1 works, 1e3 is Float while
1000 is Integer). Lexical -0, -0.0 and -0e3 become Float negative zero.
Overflow and nonzero-to-zero underflow fail; representable subnormals work.
A valid ordinary out-of-range number may be filtered or projected away; malformed
sources and invalid rich grammar cannot. Projection may normalize numbers under
existing gojq semantics; no lexical/type-fidelity promise survives it.
.PP
Queries see the actual rich grammar, not ordinary map fields. For duplicate names,
--project '[."$map"[] | select(.[0] == "name") | .[1]]' collects all matches.
An uncollected projection fails for zero/multiple results. Arbitrary projected
JSON can display but must pass rich grammar for a binary target. Ordinary sources
retain ordinary target interpretation. No projection means no identity-query
normalization, even with a filter; explicit numeric queries can lose precision.
.PP
Decoded depth 100, one million semantic values and 16 MiB str/bin/ext bytes
are independent of view depth 100, one million JSON values and 16 MiB key/string
bytes. Roots have depth zero. Keys and values count separately; view wrappers,
entries/pair arrays and decimal/base64 strings all count. Counters do not reset.
Base64/container costs are checked before allocation. Wrappers can hit the view
limit before the semantic limit, and a fitting view can exceed exact 16 MiB
serialized output. False filtering skips only that output check, never source
admission. These are not hard memory/CPU limits. A 10-16 MiB view cannot fit the
10 MiB import cap. Reconstruction is semantic, conditional on both representations
fitting their limits, never original wire-byte identity.
Ordinary selected values independently admit semantic and rich-expansion budgets;
rich selected values admit their actual JSON view and semantic budgets. Query
result limits alone do not admit a binary target.
.PP
Binary output is stdout only, explicitly selected, capped at 16 MiB and fully
staged before any external write. It contains one value without LF or ANSI.
Physical Linux/macOS terminals and Windows consoles are refused before payload
access, independently of NO_COLOR or TERM=dumb; there is no force override.
Unsupported platforms fail unsupported. Opaque embedded writers require a
caller-supplied safe sink; they are not unwrapped, closed or given new deadlines.
Write/short-write errors may leave partial output. Never redirect over the input:
the shell can truncate it before nesdit starts. No managed output, in-place edit
or backup is provided. No new Windows bounded-stop promise.
See the MessagePack guide for the full grammar, resource and lifecycle contract.
.SH PROTOBUF INSPECTION
.B nesdit --protobuf-file event.bin --descriptor-set schemas/events.pb --message-type example.Order
.PP
.B producer | nesdit --protobuf-stdin --descriptor-set schemas/events.pb --message-type example.Order
.PP
Inspect one raw binary message read-only, without a broker. Select exactly one
explicit source. The descriptor is a separate regular local binary FileDescriptorSet
containing all ordinary and option-only imports, well-known types and Any targets.
The message name is exact and fully qualified, without a leading dot or URL.
No ambient registry, network resolution, runtime compiler or binary write-back is used.
.TP
.B --protobuf-file FILE
Read one regular payload file. Repeated selections, positional inputs and a literal
- are rejected (use ./- for that filename). Commas remain literal. File mode never
reads redirected stdin. Symlinks resolving to regular files are allowed.
.TP
.B --protobuf-stdin
Explicitly read one body through EOF. False is invalid; redirected stdin and schema
options alone do not select this mode. There is no framing, prefix removal or
codec guessing. Success does not establish schema identity or absence of concatenation.
.PP
The inclusive raw cap is 10 MiB; exactly that size still requires EOF or another
byte. One byte over fails without waiting for EOF. Zero-byte EOF is a real body
and may succeed, depending on the type; missing required fields fail. Open
under-limit stdin can wait indefinitely: no standalone timer is provided.
.PP
Stdout is only the decoded/projected JSON value, including scalar and array roots,
with no source or NATS envelope, readiness banner or footer. Presentation is
pretty on a Unix terminal, compact when redirected, with one LF. NO_COLOR and
TERM=dumb disable color; Windows stays plain. Use --filter and --project against
the decoded root. Filter requires one boolean; false emits zero bytes and skips
projection. Projection requires one value and may construct new keys without
editing input; it is ordinary JSON, not a Protobuf round-trip guarantee.
.PP
Standard ProtoJSON names, presence, enum, base64-byte, decimal-string 64-bit integer
and special-float mappings apply. Well-known types and locally declared bracketed
extensions are supported. Any expands locally using its URL's final type name;
its hostname is not fetched or authenticated. Missing targets, malformed embedded
data and missing required fields inside expansions fail. Unknown wire fields are
omitted. Decoded object/map keys sort recursively; array and query-result ordering
are retained. This is not lossless binary inspection or universal canonical JSON.
.PP
Only the source, descriptor-set, message-type, filter, project and log-format flags
plus help/version are accepted. Native broker options, legacy format/output-format
(even json), pretty, query/where/from-file/arg/argjson, editing/check/dry-run/backup,
strict/keep-going, timeout and resource overrides fail even at explicit defaults.
Options, queries and descriptors are prepared before payload access.
.PP
Fixed descriptor caps: 4 MiB, 256 files, 10000 declarations, depth 100 for the
message tree and import chains. Declarations count files, messages, fields,
extensions, oneofs, enums, enum values, services and methods, including synthetic
map entries and oneofs. Options/source locations count toward depth, not declarations.
Decoded/expanded and actual-WKT-shaped JSON structures each independently allow
depth 100, 1000000 value nodes and 16 MiB key/string bytes. Roots are depth zero;
containers/scalars count nodes, keys count bytes, and bytes charge base64 length.
Any targets descend without resetting budgets and share 10 MiB aggregate embedded
wire bytes. Both shapes are assessed before full marshaling. A conservative
16 MiB serialized certificate covers escaping, numeric bounds, syntax and optional
comma spaces; it can reject a value whose actual bytes would fit. Actual output
must still fit the certificate and view budgets. Final buffered JSON has a separate
16 MiB limit including layout and LF. Inline queries retain the 1 MiB limit and
existing projection limits. These are component checks, not a heap/OOM guarantee.
.PP
One interrupt requests cancellation on Linux/macOS. Process-owned deadline-capable
stdin is unblocked; ordinary inherited blocking pipes may require the one-second
process fallback after a stop request. Forced stopping can truncate output or
omit diagnostics. Execute's borrowed I/O stays synchronous/cooperative and is
never automatically closed/deadlined; callers must arrange responsive I/O or
unblocking. No read worker is abandoned, no host exit is installed, and no new
Windows interrupt bound is claimed. Success exits 0, failure 1, interruption 130.
Read/decode/query/encoding failures emit no value; external write failure can
leave partial bytes. Diagnostics identify stages without quoting input contents.
See the Protobuf guide for full limits and cancellation qualifications.
.SH JETSTREAM HISTORY
.B nesdit --nats-history ORDERS --subject 'orders.>' --start-seq 1 --server nats://127.0.0.1:4222 --duration 30s
.PP
Read bounded retained JSON through standard leader INFO/GET, without creating,
binding, pulling or acknowledging consumers, advancing existing consumer positions,
changing stream configuration or republishing application messages. API request
publications and reply-inbox subscriptions are expected; broker accounting can change.
.TP
.B --nats-history STREAM
Select one explicit stream, mutually exclusive with Core and standalone selectors.
No positional files or stdin are read. Stream names must be safe API tokens without
dots, wildcards, path separators, whitespace or control characters.
.TP
.B --subject PATTERN
Required explicit subject pattern. Quote wildcards; > deliberately selects all.
.TP
.B --start-seq N / --start-time RFC3339
Exactly one inclusive start is required: positive uint64 sequence or timezone-qualified
stored-message time with at most nine fractional digits. Time must be within
1677-09-21T00:12:43.145224192Z through 2262-04-11T23:47:16.854775807Z inclusive,
including equivalent offset instants. Invalid bounds fail before connection, never
rounding or overflowing nanoseconds.
.TP
.B --end-seq N
Optional positive inclusive upper sequence, not below a sequence start. The initial
INFO fixes H, the last sequence; the effective upper bound never exceeds H.
.PP
Server and positive total duration are mandatory. Optional count counts validated,
in-range admissions before decode/filter, not emitted matches. Holes and nonmatches
are skipped by next-by-subject requests. Later publications never extend H; a reply
beyond H can cross the wire but is not admitted. This is retained observation, not
a snapshot, completeness guarantee, loss count or guaranteed resume facility.
.PP
Add --nats-codec protobuf plus descriptor-set and message-type for the existing
local Protobuf decoder. JSON remains the default; JSON and text forbid schema flags. Queries
see only the decoded payload root. Omitted GET data is a zero-byte body: it may be
valid Protobuf, but is not JSON. Stored Status: 503 headers are data, not Core's
bodyless sentinel. API no-responders is still an error.
.PP
Envelope order is stream, sequence (decimal string), stored_time (UTC RFC3339Nano),
subject, headers, payload. Metadata is validated broker provenance, not payload or
header names. Repeated headers and native output layouts are preserved.
.PP
History supports stable 2.15.x from 2.15.0 on standalone servers or homogeneous
same-series clusters, default account-local $JS.API only. Connected-server version
and INFO's server-generated _nats.ver are checked; missing, malformed, prerelease,
other-series or leaderless cluster responses fail closed. Homogeneity is an external
precondition, not certified for future leaders. Core has no new version restriction.
INFO/GET request-PUB and reply-inbox-SUB authority is needed; GET permission covers
the whole stream. Subject selection is not a broker-enforced subject ACL.
No direct-read, consumer, reconnect, permission-widening or scan-from-start fallback.
Verified TLS/auth and separate security-file rules remain unchanged.
.PP
Local preparation precedes the total clock; duration then covers connect, INFO,
GET, decode, queries and output. Each request has at most five seconds, capped by
remaining duration. One request is outstanding, without prefetch. History readiness
means initial INFO was validated, not a subscription or arriving record.
.PP
Empty/range/no-match exhaustion succeeds with reason=exhaustion. Only API 404/10037
means server-reported no message; missing stream 10059 and other failures fail.
The server maps storage lookup errors to 10037, so exhaustion does not prove no
internal storage fault. An active session rechecks stream identity before exhaustion,
without replacing H; detected disappearance/recreation fails. Count/duration need
no extra read. Detected fatal errors override normal stops. Before-ready expiry
fails; ready cooperative duration may leave unread or unfinished events.
.PP
INFO is capped at 1 MiB and GET at 16 MiB before JSON/base64 conversion. Decoded
payload is capped at 10 MiB, headers at 64 KiB and routing strings at 4 KiB each.
Native query/final-event and selected Protobuf budgets still apply, inclusively,
without truncation; these are not heap/OOM guarantees. Invalid protocol metadata,
duplicate keys, trailing content and simultaneous success/error fail.
.PP
History-only options fail in other modes. Legacy editing/format/resource flags fail
here, even explicit defaults. Success exits 0, failure 1, interruption 130. Native
one-second process-stop fallback and cooperative Execute qualifications apply.
See the history guide for full support, retention, permission and lifecycle limits.
.SH NATS OBSERVATION
.B nesdit --nats 'orders.>' --server nats://127.0.0.1:4222 --duration 30s --count 3
.PP
Observe live Core NATS without reading input documents or stdin. JSON is the default.
Server and positive
finite total duration are required; duration includes connection setup, not just
idle time. The optional positive count limits messages admitted for processing,
not every publication or the number successfully displayed. Admission happens
before decoding/filtering; a malformed message or false predicate consumes a slot.
No reconnect, queue group, history or publishing is performed.
File editing/query flags are rejected; use --filter and --project instead.
.TP
.B --nats-codec json|protobuf|text
Select the payload codec; defaults to json, with no guessing. Protobuf requires
both --descriptor-set FILE and --message-type NAME. Use a separate self-contained
local binary FileDescriptorSet and exact full message name, without leading dot
or URL. Include imports and Any targets locally; no registry lookup or compiler
is used. Schema options are invalid with JSON or legacy file/pipe input, and
--nats-codec is invalid without --nats or --nats-history, or with standalone Protobuf.
.PP
Schema preparation runs once under cancellation before connection and the total
duration clock. Protobuf bodies are raw, without prefix stripping or framing.
Standard ProtoJSON mapping, sorted object keys, WKT/Any/extensions and all shared
Protobuf component limits described above apply. Queries see the decoded root,
including scalar roots. Unlike standalone output, Core retains its transport
envelope; headers, layout and LF count toward the separate 16 MiB final event cap.
Ordinary zero-byte Protobuf can succeed when the type permits it; bodyless
Status: 503 fails before decoding. Malformed wire data and missing required
fields fail, not quiet success. JSON payload order remains unchanged.
.PP
.B --filter '.show == true' --project '{id: .id}'
selects and projects at the payload root: use .id, not the displayed .payload.id.
The filter must return exactly one boolean; false suppresses without warnings or
projection. Use boolean comparisons, not select(). Missing/nonboolean or
zero/multiple filter results fail. Projection must return exactly one JSON value:
objects with new/renamed keys, arrays, strings, finite numbers, booleans and null
are supported. No fan-out or zero-result suppression is provided. Transport
subject and repeated headers remain unchanged and cannot be queried.
.PP
Without filtering every decoded message matches; without projection the original
decoded payload bypasses gojq. Queries use floating-point decimal/exponent numbers
and machine/big integers, not universal numeric precision. Expressions compile
once before connection; blank, invalid or oversized expressions fail locally.
Malformed payloads fail before filtering. Query/encoding failures emit none of that
event and retain earlier output. Three false predicates with count three stop at
admitted=3, emitted=0, unfinished_event=false; emitted counts fully written events.
.PP
With the default JSON codec, each message must contain one complete JSON value.
Multiline bodies and scalars work; empty, malformed and concatenated values fail. The JSON envelope contains
subject, sorted headers with repeated-value arrays, and payload, in that order.
Payload metadata-like keys remain separate. This is a decoded view, not byte-exact
capture. Readiness and stop/error diagnostics are on stderr, with admitted/emitted
counts and an unfinished-event indication; stdout contains events only.
.PP
Use one explicit nats://host:port or tls://host:port endpoint. TLS verifies the
server certificate and hostname. URL credentials, lists, queries and fragments
are rejected; there is no insecure TLS mode.
.TP
.B --creds path/to/operator.creds
Load a combined user JWT/NKey credentials file. Requires explicit tls://.
.TP
.B --tls-ca path/to/ca.pem
Load a PEM trust bundle for this connection, replacing system roots. Omit this
flag to use system trust. Requires explicit tls://.
.TP
.B --tls-cert path/to/client.pem --tls-key path/to/client.key
Load a matching PEM client certificate/private-key pair for mutual TLS. Both
flags must be supplied together and require explicit tls://. May be combined
with JWT credentials.
.PP
Security files must be readable regular files, at most 1 MiB each. Empty paths,
missing pairs, malformed credentials, invalid/empty CA bundles and invalid or
mismatched key pairs fail before connecting. Credential container/user-key syntax
is checked locally; the broker verifies JWT signatures, trust, expiry and
permissions. Protect private files with restrictive permissions.
Files are preloaded before the duration clock starts; no handshake callback reads
them again. First-interrupt handling includes preparation. Diagnostics identify
flags/stages without paths, file contents, keys, JWTs or raw TLS errors.
Token/user-password-only, TLS-first-only, proxy, WebSocket and NATS context registry
configurations are unsupported. Local tests do not establish actual-cluster
compatibility; use an authorized bounded smoke test without weakening security.
.PP
.B --nats-output auto
selects two-space pretty JSON on a Unix stdout terminal and JSONL when redirected.
.B --nats-output jsonl
forces compact layout;
.B --nats-output pretty
writes consecutive multiline JSON documents, not JSONL. Redirected output stays
plain. NO_COLOR and TERM=dumb disable color, not pretty layout. Windows stays plain.
.PP
Observation is at-most-once. Readiness is a processing barrier, not a complete
delivery guarantee. A quiet ready duration is successful; expiration before
readiness fails. Detected disconnection, pending overflow, malformed data, resource
or output errors fail immediately and retain earlier complete output.
.PP
Fixed limits: 10 MiB raw payload, 1000 JSON container depth, eight pending messages and
64 MiB pending payload-only bytes, 64 KiB admitted wire headers, 4 KiB each subject
and reply, 1 MiB per inline expression, and 16 MiB serialized output including
whitespace and final newline. Raw projection results are checked before recursive
conversion: depth 1000, 1,000,000 container/scalar value nodes and 16 MiB aggregate
key/string bytes. Query compilation, Protobuf schema and security-file preparation precede the
session timer; evaluation and
result checks share the session deadline and cancellation.
At-limit values are allowed. Client frame/header allocations precede application
checks; these are component limits, not a whole-process memory bound. Use a trusted,
correctly configured broker. Expressions are trusted code for resource purposes:
gojq may allocate before yielding, so result/output caps are not a strict query
heap limit or evaluator sandbox. Cancellation is not uniformly preemptible inside
builtins or reconciliation. Output contains application data and is not sanitized.
.PP
One interrupt requests cancellation on Linux/macOS. The first stop request starts
a one-second cleanup allowance; a CLI-only forced-exit fallback can truncate the
final event, skip cleanup and omit final diagnostics if output or other work blocks.
Check exit status and validate redirected output. In-process Execute never exits
its host and requires responsive writers. No new Windows interrupt bound is claimed.
.PP
Normal count or cooperative ready-duration completion exits 0. Duration before
emission may discard an unfinished event with an explicit indication and exit 0;
incomplete emission and forced stops exit 1. Interruption exits 130. Earlier fatal
errors are not erased by the deadline. Existing file-mode exit meanings are unchanged.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Error (bad query, unreadable file, parse failure, etc.).
.TP
.B 130
Native observation or standalone inspection interrupted or cancelled
(Linux/macOS CLI interrupt handling).
.TP
.B 2
Drift detected: the query would change the input (only when
.B \-\-check
is used).
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Print the value of a key to stdout:
.PP
.B "    nesdit --query '.database.host' config.yaml"
.PP
Edit a key in-place:
.PP
.B "    nesdit -i --query '.database.host = ""db.example.com""' config.yaml"
.PP
Preview changes as a unified diff without writing:
.PP
.B "    nesdit -n --query '.version = ""2.0.0""' package.json"
.PP
Gate a CI step — exit 2 if config has drifted from expected state:
.PP
.B "    nesdit --check --query '.env == ""production""' deploy.toml"
.PP
Read from STDIN and write to STDOUT (pipe usage):
.PP
.B "    cat config.json | nesdit --query '.settings.debug = true'"
.PP
Batch-edit multiple files in-place, skipping errors:
.PP
.B "    nesdit -i --keep-going --query '.version = ""1.1.0""' configs/*.yaml"
